# format specifiers = A way to format strings by inserting values into placeholders within a string. The placeholders are defined using curly braces {} and can be replaced with values using the format() method or f-strings.

# .(format) method = A method that allows you to format strings by replacing placeholders with values. The placeholders are defined using curly braces {} and can be replaced with values by passing them as arguments to the format() method.
# :(f-string) = A way to format strings by inserting values directly into the string using curly braces {}. The values can be variables, expressions, or even function calls.
# :02 = A format specifier that formats a number to have at least 2 digits, padding with zeros if necessary.
# :< = A format specifier that left-aligns the text within the specified width.
# :> = A format specifier that right-aligns the text within the specified width.
# :^ = A format specifier that centers the text within the specified width.
# :, = A format specifier that adds a comma as a thousand separator in numbers.
# :.2f = A format specifier that formats a floating-point number to have 2 decimal places.
# :+ = A format specifier that adds a plus sign before positive numbers and a minus sign before negative numbers.
# := = A format specifier that places the sign before the number, even if the number is positive.
# :b = A format specifier that formats a number as binary.
# : = inserts a space before positive numbers and a minus sign before negative numbers.